{
  "candidate": {
    "commit": "48733052d6a678ae00ec1064c5d9ef445135e45f",
    "tree": "268e019edb47e8f6f10194d22bc3b5682bbc592c"
  },
  "dispatchId": "ALFRED-READINESS-20261007",
  "findings": [
    {
      "code": "DESIGN-R7-STALE-COUNTS",
      "severity": "blocking",
      "summary": "design.md's 2026-10-06 device-switch amendment contradicts Spec §22.0, PRD §15 and traceability. It says 'seventeen hurdles', 'twelve rows are owned by eight contracts' and a '17-row map'. The other sources fix 20 rows (T1–T20): 15 owned by R7 across ten contracts (R7-1…R7-8, R7-10, R7-11) and 5 mapped. design.md also omits T18–T20, PO decisions 20–26, R7-10 (stale authoring registration), R7-11 (parallel dispatch) and the §22.12 sequencing. The canonical integrated design therefore misstates the scope the PO is asked to approve. Spec R7-9b's static count check covers only the Spec §22.0 table and the traceability map, so it would not catch this."
    },
    {
      "code": "R7-10-BRANCH-A-NO-PO-DECISION",
      "severity": "blocking",
      "summary": "Spec §22.10 branch (A) lets an agent supersede, without a signature, an authoring registration of an earlier revision whose owner is in ANY state, including `live` and `unavailable`. The Spec says PO decisions #22 and #23 'widen the eligible set', and traceability attributes T8 to them. But #22 covers only same-digest registrations with an `unobserved` owner, and #23 covers only another lineage or a later revision with a positively not-live/ended owner. No recorded PO decision covers branch (A). It affects a live owner's work and is in tension with §20 (owners are never reclaimed by inference) and the PRD §15 non-goal of not weakening §20. The 'non-destructive' justification is the design's own reasoning, not PO authority. Either record the PO decision or narrow branch (A)."
    },
    {
      "code": "R7-6-CHECK-VS-R3-ONE-COMMAND",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "The R7-6 pre-signing readiness check hands the PO a second terminal command before the signing command, released only after a recorded observation. Spec §21.0 says the R3-1 inventory 'counts any request for a PO terminal command as a PO interaction'. §21.3 and R3-5 say 'one command per decision'. R7-6d(iv) asserts that no decision or class is added, but it does not reconcile this with those two rules or say how the R3-1 inventory classifies the check. The release mechanism (how the agent learns the check passed and then hands over the signing command) is left to the implementation, which leaves the AC-37/R3-1 interplay under-specified."
    },
    {
      "code": "ADVISOR-EXCEPTION-RATIONALE-SCOPE",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "The proposed Advisor-unavailable exception rationale goes beyond what Spec §21.4/R4-1 allow, which is receipt-backed facts only (course outcome and code, absence of a native child). It adds narrative about the device switch, decisions 17–26, readiness, and 'Delivering that route is part of the approved scope', although the design is not yet approved. Only the failure receipt (code native-initial-answer-provenance-unavailable, childStarted false, inputSubmitted false, attemptCount 0) is backed by evidence. The exception is correctly non-authorizing, and no Advisor PASS or disposition exists. The PO must treat the design as having no Advisor answer."
    },
    {
      "code": "INPUT-NOT-VERBATIM",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "The 'original user input' source (design-input.md) is a consolidated index. It is not preserved original bytes: PO decisions of 2026-10-03 through 2026-10-07 are 'recorded in distilled form, not as a transcript', and it admits it is not raw-input evidence. Only the recovery sentence is verbatim. This review can check coverage of the distilled decisions but cannot independently confirm fidelity to the PO's actual words. PRD §11 requires preserved, digest-bound original input, and the package states that the final workflow must bind it."
    },
    {
      "code": "CURRENT-EVIDENCE-IN-IGNORED-ROOT",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "This revision's own course artifacts (initial context, failure receipt) sit under the ignored root `evidence/design-course/...`. R7-3 forbids exactly that for approval-bound artifacts (the T5 defect), so the revision-5 approval could again be unverifiable on a third device until R7-3 ships. The package does not state this self-application or any interim mitigation, such as committing a tracked copy before approval."
    },
    {
      "code": "STALE-DECISION-COUNTS-PRD",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "PRD §14 refers to design-input '(fifteen items)' for 2026-10-04 and PRD §10 maps 'decisions #1–#15', but design-input and traceability record 16 decisions (#1–#16). PRD §10's traceability table has no row for the 2026-10-06/07 decisions 17–26 or R7/Spec §22. The PRD header 'Current amendment' and §8 A-5 scale-risk text list R1–R6 and recovery but not R7. design-input.md 'Scope authority' also does not name the device-switch amendment. These are documentation inconsistencies, with the normative content present in §15/§22."
    },
    {
      "code": "ACCEPTANCE-MD-NOT-IN-PACKAGE",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "Traceability maps every item to AC-26…AC-37, and the PRD/design defer completion to acceptance.md. acceptance.md is not one of the five reviewed sources, and no evidence shows AC-37 and the AC-35/AC-36 extensions exist there. Coverage was checked only through the Spec's R-cases (R1-1…R7-11e), not through the AC rows."
    },
    {
      "code": "R7-11-R4-K3-9-OVERLAP",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "R7-11 (T19: bounded wait on the dispatch-budget counter lock, `counter-lock-timeout`) and R4 K3-9 (cross-platform owner identity for the same lock, R4-4) both change guard-dispatch-budget.mjs. They are sequenced in different steps: R7-11 in step 1 of §21.7, per §22.12, and R4 in step 2. The Spec states no ordering or interface between the two live/dead/ambiguous owner semantics and the new wait path, so the same-lock changes could conflict."
    },
    {
      "code": "UNVERIFIED-EXTERNAL-CLAIMS",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "Spec §22.1 and §22.7 assert upstream facts: git-for-windows/git#6449, the fix in 2.56.0.windows.2, and that Git for Windows maps `/dev/null` in both its MSVCRT and UCRT builds. §22.6 cites 'independent Critic rounds 1 to 3 of 2026-10-07' as design rationale. None is evidenced in the package, and design-input says no Critic review is claimed. R7-1a verifies only a stub git plus 'every dialect the fixture host offers', so a real 2.56.0.windows.1 or both Windows builds may go untested."
    },
    {
      "code": "R7-6F-NOT-RUN-FAILS-MATRIX",
      "severity": "non-blocking",
      "summary": "R7-6f says a host that cannot create the unreadable-directory condition reports `not-run`, which fails the matrix and never skips. The Spec does not say how the required win32/POSIX matrix is satisfied on a host that structurally cannot produce that condition (for example a Windows ACL or root-run POSIX). The acceptance may be unsatisfiable without a stated alternative or a designated host."
    }
  ],
