    "changedFiles": [],
    "text": "INTERIM / NOT DONE. Orientation consumed the whole working budget (36 of 45 calls) and the budget checkpoint fired before the first edit. No source file changed, nothing committed, no verify run. TCP039 is still red. Findings a follow-up can build on directl<machine-path> TCP039 is the case named 'R7-7c' (toolchain-preflight.test.mjs:571). r77Run spawns each script with cwd = a fresh git-init fixture repo and HOME/GIT_CONFIG redirected; text = stdout + newline + stderr. r77JsonObjects accepts only (a) lines that start with '{' and parse as one-line JSON, or (b) the whole text as one JSON document. So the blocked prepare-for-signature output must be COMPACT one-line JSON on stdout (pretty-printed JSON plus any stderr text parses as neither) with a top-level environmentReadiness key. The whole text must not contain the strings signIntentCommand, authorizeBySignatureCommand, expiresAt. bootstrap and prepare findings must deepEqual runEnvironmentReadinessReport({rootDir: repo}).findings.\n\n2. pipeline-start-preflight.mjs: do NOT add the field inside observePipelineStartPreflight (pipeline-start-preflight.test.mjs:282 pins the exact key set). Add it in main() (line 1848), which is sync and has no importer anywhere (verified by git grep): make main async, await a dynamic import of ./toolchain-preflight.mjs inside try/catch, call runEnvironmentReadinessReport({rootDir: process.cwd()}), spread {...result, environmentReadiness} into the ONE compact stdout line for every status (the fixture repo is most likely on the early-return governance-inactive path, so do not restrict to status ready). Failure isolation: on any throw emit {schema:'pipeline.environment-readiness.v1', mutation:false, nonGating:true, ok:false, findings:[], unavailable:<code>}. Exit-code logic unchanged. Entry line 1884 becomes main().then(code => { process.exitCode = code; }). The bootstrapPayload receipt already measures only a subset of the emitted envelope (it excludes cloneProvisioning), so leave it.\n\n3. guard-human-override.mjs: main(argv, io, options) is exported and SYNC, and guard-human-override.test.mjs:705-800 and 809-888 call it in-process for prepare-for-signature with no readiness option, expecting status 0 with an unset key directory. Gating therefore must not default on inside main(). Design: add an options.environmentReadiness seam (a precomputed report object). In the prepare-for-signature branch, after flag validation and BEFORE the prepareHumanGuardOverrideForSignature call (line 402): if a report was supplied and any of signing-toolchain, po-key-directory, trust-anchor-match is missing or status !== 'ok', write compact one-line JSON {schema:'pipeline.human-guard-override-prepare-for-signature.v1', status:'blocked', code, root, requestSha256, blockedBy:[ids], repairs:[{findingId,status,repair}], environmentReadiness} to stdout, one human line to stderr that names none of the three withheld keys, return 2, and never call prepare (so no window starts). Success path writes {...prepared, environmentReadiness}. The process entry (line 522) becomes async: when argv[0] is prepare-for-signature, parse flags(argv.slice(1)), await runEnvironmentReadinessReport({rootDir: parsed.repo}) via dynamic import (failure yields findings:[] so the three ids are missing and the gate fails closed), then main(argv, {}, {environmentReadiness}).\n\n4. Test portability risk (not fixable by me, tests are the contract): r77Run does text.split(repo).join('<fixture>') on the raw text. On win32 the JSON-escaped backslash path survives, on POSIX the '--root <repo>' argv element of the git-hooks repair is rewritten and deepEqual against the direct report would fail. Likely passes on this Windows machine only.\n\n5. No protected-path refusal was encountered (no edit was attempted). One oddity: a Bash git grep with a ':!' pathspec was refused by guard-push ('push target is not unambiguous').\n\nDoD: all not run / not verifiable. verify: pending. independent review: pending. PO acceptance: open."
  }
}

